  "source_dossier": {
    "summary_short": "This consensus critique argues that mindfulness research is promising but overhyped because definitions, measures, interventions, safety reporting, and neuroimaging claims are often too loose. It recommends sharper constructs, stronger controls, active harm monitoring, open-science replication, and more modest public communication.",
    "summary_long": "Mind the Hype evaluates mindfulness and meditation research at a moment when the topic had moved from a fringe scientific interest into psychotherapy, education, workplaces, military resilience programs, and mass media. The authors' central concern is that the public, clinicians, journalists, and some researchers often treat mindfulness as a single well-defined object, even though the term covers many practices, states, traits, interventions, and measurement instruments. This semantic breadth makes it easy to overgeneralize from a questionnaire score, a short app-based exercise, a structured MBSR course, or a long retreat to mindfulness as such. The paper therefore frames definition and methodology as inseparable: without specifying what was practiced, taught, measured, and compared, evidence cannot be interpreted reliably.\n\nThe clinical discussion is similarly cautious. The article recognizes selective promise, especially around MBCT for depressive relapse and some symptoms such as anxiety, depression, and pain, but it rejects the idea that the clinical evidence is broadly settled. Much research remains early-stage, relies on wait-list or treatment-as-usual comparisons, and includes little ordinary-practice evidence. Interventions labeled mindfulness-based differ in content, duration, delivery mode, instructor training, homework, and target population, so broad efficacy claims can mask major differences in dose and mechanism.\n\nA major contribution is the paper's treatment of safety. It challenges assumptions that meditation has minimal adverse effects simply because harms have not been rigorously studied. Case reports and observational studies describe serious or distressing experiences, including psychosis, mania, depersonalization, anxiety, panic, traumatic-memory reexperiencing, and deterioration. The authors do not claim these reports establish incidence or simple causality, but they argue that adverse events require prospective monitoring, better measurement, informed consent, and attention to contraindications.\n\nThe neuroimaging discussion extends the same anti-hype stance. Brain images and group-level findings can appear authoritative, but they rest on complex analytic pipelines and can be affected by cross-sectional designs, participant-selection effects, head motion, cardiorespiratory differences, and unclear practical significance. The article allows that meta-analyses suggest modest structural and functional brain changes, while warning that similar changes occur after other forms of skill acquisition and should not be translated prematurely into clinical prescriptions. Overall, the source is a reform agenda for contemplative science: use sharper constructs, stronger controls, adversarial and interdisciplinary collaboration, preregistration, open replications, adverse-event monitoring, and more careful communication with the public.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Central Argument",
        "body": "The article's central argument is that mindfulness research is promising but overextended. Public and scientific language often compresses many practices, states, interventions, and measures into one label, which lets preliminary findings travel farther than their designs justify. The authors treat conceptual clarity as a prerequisite for trustworthy evidence rather than a philosophical side issue.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "0",
          "1",
          "10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Measurement Problem",
        "body": "Mindfulness questionnaires and related measures do not yet solve the construct problem. Different instruments draw on different traditions and factor structures, and self-reports can be shaped by expectations, social desirability, and the difficulty of verifying first-person states from third-person evidence. The proposed remedy is to measure specific supporting functions and behaviors instead of chasing one universal mindfulness score.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "2",
          "3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Clinical Evidence",
        "body": "The clinical evidence is portrayed as selective and preliminary. MBCT and related interventions may help some people in some circumstances, but only a small share of MBI research has progressed to active-control or real-world stages. Because intervention packages vary widely, the article warns against treating all MBI outcomes as evidence for a single therapeutic method.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "4",
          "5",
          "6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Safety And Adverse Effects",
        "body": "The source gives unusual weight to harms. Serious or distressing meditation-related experiences appear in case reports and observational studies, while many trials lack methods capable of estimating adverse-event frequency. The authors argue that safety guidance should move beyond exclusion and boilerplate cautions toward prospective monitoring, clinician interviews, and clearer contraindication practices.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "7",
          "8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Neuroimaging Limits",
        "body": "The neuroimaging section cautions against treating brain images as direct evidence of practical benefit. Group averages, cross-sectional comparisons, physiological artifacts, and analytic choices can all shape observed differences. Even where meta-analyses indicate modest brain changes, the source stresses that comparable changes can follow other learned skills, so practical and clinical significance remain open questions.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Research Agenda",
        "body": "The agenda is prescriptive rather than dismissive. The authors call for precise terminology, stronger active controls, interdisciplinary and even adversarial collaborations, preregistration, open-science replications, direct adverse-event assessment, and more accurate communication with journalists and the public. The intended outcome is better contemplative science, not less inquiry into meditation.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "3",
          "8",
          "10"
        ]
      }
    ]
